      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hamlet began as Hudson, with its first post office opening on February 21, 1890.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hamlet_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In order to avoid repetition with another Hudson in the state, the town was renamed Hamlet in 1904 as it constituted a rural hamlet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamlet%2C_Nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hamlet’s first settlers filed homestead claims along Red Willow Creek in 1874, after the Union Pacific Railroad arrived in the late 1860s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hamlet_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the 1920s, Hamlet boomed thanks to a new depot and the construction of Union Church in 1921.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/hamlet_nebraska"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Daniel near Hamlet was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1985, highlighting the area’s 19th‑century settlement patterns.",
